\subsection{At $B=50$, a re-drawn background changes the top-3 set more often than a re-run of the \texttt{shap} default}
\label{sec:floors}
Figure~\ref{fig:floors} compares three noise floors in one metric, the agreement on the top-3 set of two independent draws, on the same instances within a cell.
Two runs of the explainer at the reference budget agree for $0.91$--$0.99$ of the pairs in every non-linear cell shown; at a small budget ($m=64$ for German Credit and ACS Employment, $51$ for ACS Income and GMSC, $25$ for Pima) for $0.62$--$0.90$.
Two independent backgrounds of $50$ random rows, each explained exactly, agree for $0.27$--$0.69$.
Retraining gives three floors that we do not pool, since they answer different questions: a bootstrap resample of the training rows ($0.14$--$0.73$), a different seed with the same data ($0.50$--$0.88$) and perturbed hyperparameters ($0.14$--$0.70$; ranges exclude the German MLP), each over models whose held-out AUC is within $0.01$ of the reference model's.
They rest on one trained model per population and $K=10$ retrained models, fewer where the AUC filter kept fewer (open markers: ACS Income MLP bootstrap $K=3$, German boosting $8$ and logistic regression $9$); for the German MLP the AUC filter kept $1$ of $24$ bootstrap, $3$ of $24$ hyperparameter and $6$ of $15$ seed retrainings, so its retraining floors are not available, and the three numbers are the weakest of the section.
In every non-linear cell the explainer floor at the reference budget is above the background floor and above all three retraining floors.
For logistic regression the explainer floor is $1$, the seed-only floor $1$, and the background, bootstrap and hyperparameter floors are $0.41$--$0.67$, $0.23$--$0.83$ and $0.22$--$0.79$.
At $B=50$, then, an instance's top-3 set changes more under another background of the same size, or under a retrained model, than under the randomness of the \texttt{shap} default.
This is not so for every estimator: the paired Kernel estimator, which is noisier at the same budget (agreement $0.29$--$0.73$ at the reference budget), agrees with itself less than two backgrounds do on Pima, on ACS Income random forest and on GMSC boosting, and more on GMSC random forest and MLP; at the reference budget the paired difference excludes zero in $7$ of the $13$ non-linear cells that have floor-(b) data under this comparison (at the largest sampled budget, $m=16384$, floor (b) is lower also on German Credit and ACS Employment).
The background floor depends on the size of the background: the median over the $18$ cells with all four backgrounds of the top-3 agreement of two independent backgrounds ($K=10$ each, exact attributions) is $0.46$ at $B=20$, $0.61$ at $B=50$, $0.75$ at $B=200$ and $0.77$ for $50$ $k$-means medoids.
It thus mixes the finite size of the background, which shrinks as $B$ grows, with the choice of reference distribution, which does not (Appendix~\ref{app:background}), and we read it at $B=50$ only.

